`run_context.hpp`: the one function every context goes through, for `ca::with`,
`ca::context_manager` and `ca::ctx` alike.

`run_context<E>(handles, enter, leave)` pushes a recovery point (layer 1, `impl::recovery`), runs
`enter`, pops the point and calls `leave(error, outcome)` with nullptr on success or the error
otherwise. The error is what `enter` returned when it returned a failed `result<void, E>`, or
what a trap it took left on the point: the failed result's own error when the trap came from
reading one (an E taken whole, moved when it cannot be copied, else its `ca::error` made an E),
or `errc::not_recoverable` with the trap's reason in a new trace frame (`traced_error`).

`handles` is `takes_every_trap` for a context without one: the point then has no `_handles`, and
the trap machinery takes every trap and passes every `ca::ask` for it. Otherwise the point
carries `decide_context` (handles on a `ca::trapped`) and `unwind_context`, which runs `leave`
with outcome `passed` while a trap goes past: with the trap's E as it is, else its `ca::error`
made an E and cleaned up after. `traced_error` records the reason in the trace once, on the trap,
so every context the trap meets sees the same error. `leave_state` calls `Self::leave` with the
outcome when it takes one.

`is_context_error<E>` admits the error types a context may fail with: `ca::error`, or a type made
from one through `E::of(error)` (as `ca::report` is) or a constructor.

`enter_recovery` is the function that calls `__builtin_setjmp`. It is `gnu::noipa` on gcc: when the
body never returns normally, gcc's interprocedural analysis would otherwise conclude that the
landing path is the only exit and drop the body's stores before a trap, which the caller then
would not see. Clang only needs it out of line.
